course: dictionary
lesson: 12241
title: Consolidate: stronger or joined together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Consolidate is a verb, and it has two meanings. The first is to make something stronger, firmer or more secure. «Practise your times tables every day to consolidate what you have learned.» The practice makes your learning firmer, so it stays in your memory.
explain: The second meaning is to join things into one. «The two small schools will consolidate into one big school next year.» Two schools become one, so consolidate means join together. Ask yourself: is something being made stronger, or are separate things being joined? That tells you which meaning fits.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the hard word. «The team scored again to consolidate their lead.» Their lead gets stronger and safer, so the first meaning fits here. If your meaning makes the sentence silly, try the other meaning.
fact: Consolidate is a verb: consolidate, consolidates, consolidated, consolidating.
fact: «Consolidate» means to make something stronger, firmer or more secure.
fact: «Consolidate» also means to join things into one.
fact: In school, teachers set work to consolidate what you have learned, such as practice with times tables.
fact: In business and news, companies consolidate when they merge into one company.
exercise: «Practise your spellings every night to consolidate what you have learned.» What does consolidate mean here? | *Make your learning stronger and firmer | Test it once and then stop | Copy it out in a new notebook | Forget what you learned quickly
exercise: «The two small clubs will consolidate into one big club.» What does consolidate mean here? | Argue loudly about the rules | *Join together to form one club | Split into three smaller clubs | Close down for ever
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to consolidate when it means make firmer? | Weaken | Scatter | *Strengthen | Forget
test: «The company plans to consolidate its three shops into one large store.» What does consolidate mean here? | Sell the shops one at a time | Close all three shops for good | *Join the three shops into one | Paint the three shops new colours
test: Mrs Patel says, «Let's consolidate our lesson with a quick quiz.» What is she trying to do? | *Make what you learned stronger in your memory | Move the lesson to another room | Give everyone a prize | End the lesson early
exam: Which sentence uses consolidate to mean join things into one? | «I will consolidate my learning by practising.» | *«The two teams will consolidate into one squad.» | «Please consolidate the spelling list next week.» | «The team will consolidate its lead by scoring again.»
